index b8b6fd67f1..355bc7598b 100644
--- a/docs/OPTIONS-REGISTRY.md
+++ b/docs/OPTIONS-REGISTRY.md
@@ -86,6 +86,8 @@ safe measurement posture.
 | `--spec-draft-adaptive` | **OFF.** CLI flag; sizes each draft from a per-sequence EMA of accepted tokens instead of a fixed `--spec-draft-n-max`, which becomes a safety ceiling rather than a tuning parameter. Backing off is averaged (alpha 0.25, ~4-step memory); probing upward is additive. Reset on `begin()` — a new prompt or a reused server slot — never mid-generation. | Scope: `common/speculative.cpp` draft-length selection. Sits in the `else` of this fork's existing DFlash2 adaptive selector, so a controller already armed keeps precedence; the MTP sites keep their own adaptive precedence. Speculation stays distribution-preserving, so the flag can move throughput and **cannot** move output. | Turn it on where content type varies within a session and no single fixed depth is right — its author's motivating measurement is that the correct depth is *opposite* for prose and for JSON. Sweep it against your own fixed depth before adopting. | **UNMEASURED ON RIG-A — no A/B for this flag exists on this hardware, and none should be quoted.** The measured table in the source commit was taken on a **Radeon 8060S** by the upstream author (three drafter configurations × prose/json × fixed-vs-adaptive, 300 greedy tokens on a Qwen3.8-27B ROCmFP4-FAST target). Those are **upstream-reported numbers for a different GPU** and are deliberately not restated here as results of this fork. Default OFF for that reason alone: the mechanism is sound and the evidence for it is somebody else's box. |
 | `GGML_ARIFI_IQ3_MMVQ` | **DEFAULT `v2` on ALL devices — flipped 2026-09-18 (lane-254/R66 step 2b, President ruling: a proven-safe gain ships ON, including one that shows nothing on our GPU).** It is bit-identical sha-for-sha to `legacy` and 3.5x cheaper in compiled instructions per column; it buys nothing on gfx1103, whose driver already hoists the select, and it ships for the drivers that do not. Only the exact string `legacy` restores the inherited nest; anything else warns on stderr and falls back to `v2`. Specialization constant 3 (`ARIFI_IQ3_SIGN_HOIST`) of `mul_mat_vec_iq3_s.comp` and `mul_mat_vec_iq3_xxs.comp`, read once at pipeline creation, so **one binary serves both arms** and the host prints the resolved mode. Added 2026-09-18 (lane-249/R61 step 2). | Scope: the application of the iq3 sign bits, and nothing else. Both shaders already hoist the grid lookup and the sign byte out of the `NUM_COLS` loop; only the *application* of the sign — an `OpSelect` inside an `fma` argument, for a value identical in every column — was still re-evaluated per weight per column. `v2` computes the eight signed grid values once per `(l, row)` and leaves the column body as eight unguarded `fma`s. Applied to **both** iq3_s column bodies (the `ARIFI_TPB16` split-width one and the stock `sum[NUM_COLS]` one) and passed to all six registrations including the two `mul_mat_vec_id` mirrors, which are width 1 and where the hoist is arithmetically a no-op. The legacy arm is kept as **verbatim text** so the default path's codegen is provably unchanged. | **Do not turn it on for speed on this device — there is none.** It is retained for (a) any device or shape that *is* instruction-issue bound, where it is strictly cheaper at zero correctness cost, and (b) as the reference implementation of the pattern. | **Compiled cost: a real, large win. Wall clock: none.** Marginal cost of one extra column, `spirv-opt` with `--freeze-spec-const --fold-spec-const-op-composite -O`: iq3_s **1229 instr / 88 `OpSelect` → 349 / 8**; iq3_xxs **1227 / 88 → 347 / 8**; the `iq4_xs` control counted with identical flags is 334 / 8. Both iq3 shaders go from ~3.7x iq4_xs per column to **1.04x**. At `NUM_COLS = 1` the two arms compile to an identical module. Against that, the 6-round paired table at m=248320 reads v2/legacy **0.991–1.013 at every n for both types**, spread 0.6–12%, wins 1/6–5/6 — i.e. nothing, in either direction. Ship gate (`v2` wins at every n in {4,5,6,8}, never loses at n≤3): **FAIL at both types and all three shapes.** Conclusion: the iq3 mat-vec at these shapes is not instruction-issue bound. **Bit-identical, CHECKED**: legacy, a same-arm legacy control and v2 all produce sha `271472A9…C993` over a 79,258,240-byte dump of the whole iq3 `MUL_MAT` sweep spanning n=1..8. Correctness RC=0 OK both arms on `MUL_MAT` and on the 8 `MUL_MAT_ID` mirror cases. Receipts `r61-evidence/{21-spirv-counts-v2,22-isa-fold-s62,44-dump-sha256,50-perf-table-s62}.txt`; report `OPUS-R61-IQ3-MMVQ-WIDE-REPORT.md` §2(a), §2(c), §2(d). |
 | `GGML_ARIFI_IQ3_N7_ROWS` | **DEFAULT `2` on AMD RDNA3 (gfx110x), the inherited `rm_iq` (4) everywhere else — flipped 2026-09-18 (lane-254/R66 step 2c).** The spill this clears is a driver-ALLOCATOR effect measured on this device class only, so the default is device-scoped through the same `is_rdna3` probe `rm_int_n`/`rm_id` already use; every other device keeps the upstream shape. Accepts `1`, `2`, `4`; anything else warns and falls back to the default. One startup line names the resolved value and its source: `ggml_vulkan: iq3 mat-vec n=7 rows: 2 (device-probe (RDNA3)) [GGML_ARIFI_IQ3_N7_ROWS]`. Added 2026-09-18 (lane-249/R61 step 2). | Scope: `NUM_ROWS` — and the matching workgroup denominator — of the **`NUM_COLS == 7` iq3 mat-vec pipelines**, `iq3_s` AND `iq3_xxs`, f32 and f16 activations alike (`iq3_rows(i)` returns the override solely when `i + 1 == 7`). No other width, no other type, no other op. The **`MUL_MAT_ID` mirrors can never see it**: they take `rm_iq` with `NUM_COLS` fixed at 1. Built as a probe of the n=7 cliff and it turned out to be the fix. | **This is the row worth acting on in R61, not the one above it.** Turn it to `2` where iq3_s mat-vec is dispatched at width 7 — i.e. speculative verify at draft widths that produce 7-token batches. Inert everywhere else, so the cost of setting it wrongly is zero. `1` also clears the spill but cuts parallelism too far; prefer `2`. | **Measured WIN, 22.1x at n=7** (CHECK-R61-FABLE F7 struck the 23.8x headline: it rests on a single high `unset` run of 497,199 us; the 6-round legacy median is 462,277). The n=7 cliff is a **register spill**, read straight off the AMD driver via `GGML_VK_PIPELINE_STATS`: `scratchMemUsageInBytes` is **768 at n=7 and 0 at every other width**, on both `IQ3_MMVQ` arms. (n=8 uses *more* registers — 255, the RDNA3 maximum — and does not spill, so the cliff is an allocator/occupancy decision, not a shortage.) Setting `NUM_ROWS` to 1 or 2 cuts live `temp[7][NUM_ROWS]` state and takes scratch to **0** (VGPRs 177/181, beside n=6's 180); the explicit `4` arm reproduces the default spill and is the control. Wall clock at m=248320: iq3_s n=7 **~462,000 → 20,898 us/run at `=2`, a 22.1x improvement**, cliff ratio **22.5x → 1.02x (flat)**; `=1` gives 30,115 (1.46x); the `=4` control reads 461,993 (22.60x). iq3_xxs 22,189 → 17,706 (1.44x → 1.13x). Every other width unchanged. **Baseline note:** the N7_ROWS arms are single runs, and the `unset` and `=4` cells are the same pipeline by construction yet read 497,199 and 461,993 — a 7.6% spread that is the precision bound on these cells. The gain is quoted against the **6-round median of 462,277** from the ship-gate table, not against the more favourable `unset` cell (which would read 23.8x). The `=2`-over-`=1` preference rests on a single comparison and is a recommendation, not a measured law. Cliff magnitude on the shipped default: **22.5x** at m=248320, 21.6x at m=17408, 24.7x at m=5120, and it is an iq3_s phenomenon (iq3_xxs 1.38x, iq4_xs none). **Bit-identical, CHECKED**: all four arms re-run at m=248320 on identical deterministic data pass 2/2 and produce byte-identical 71,778,304-byte dumps, sha `9E70CA84…`. Receipts `r61-evidence/{46-stats-fold,46-stats-n7rows-fold,47-n7rows-fold}.txt`; report §2(b), §2(c). |
+| `GGML_ARIFI_Q6K_F32_ROWS` | **Unset = the inherited shape: `rm_kq` (2 on RDNA3) at every NUM_COLS, byte-for-byte what the tree served before.** Accepts `<1\|2\|4>` for every width or `<1\|2\|4>@<n>` for NUM_COLS ≥ n; anything else warns and keeps the default. One startup line names the resolved value: `ggml_vulkan: q6_k f32 mat-vec rows: 2 at every width (device-probe (upstream shape)) [GGML_ARIFI_Q6K_F32_ROWS]`. Added 2026-09-21 (lane-258/R74). | Scope: `NUM_ROWS` — and the matching workgroup denominator, which must move with it — of the **q6_K f32 mat-vec pipelines** `mul_mat_vec_q6_k_f32_f32` and `mul_mat_vec_q6_k_f16_f32`. No other type, no other op; the `MUL_MAT_ID` mirrors take `rm_id` with NUM_COLS fixed at 1 and can never see it. | A measurement lever: it is what lets ONE binary vary NUM_ROWS at a FIXED NUM_COLS on this shader, the way `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_WIDE_ROWS_FROM` does for the integer-dot family. Leave it unset for serving. | **Measured, and it does NOT ship a default.** R74 timed rows 1 against the shipped rows 2 over 5 shapes × 8 widths × 6 counterbalanced rounds: it admits at n=6 on 248320×5120 (1.0552, 6/6) and 1024×5120 (1.0383, 5/6) and LOSES at n=6 on the other three shapes (0.9286 / 0.9685 / 0.9827), so no shippable cell exists — NUM_ROWS is fixed at pipeline creation and carries no shape term. Registers (driver `GGML_VK_PIPELINE_STATS`): rows 1 reads 56/81/108/114/134/154/170/189 VGPRs over n=1..8 against the default 77/103/138/136/139/175/191/245, scratch 0 in both; the `4` arm is the control and SPILLS (32 B at n=6, 48 B at n=8). Bit-identity is unproven by sha, not disproven: the mandated same-arm control failed (33 of 20,254,706 f32 values differ between two runs of the unchanged arm) while the rows-1 arm differs from that control in 4 values. Receipts `r74-evidence/{10-stats-table,21-paired,45-dump-control-diff}.txt`; report §0, §2. |
+| `GGML_ARIFI_Q6K_MMVQ_ROWS` | **Unset = the inherited `rm_int_n(1*rm_kq_int, i)`: 1 row up to NUM_COLS 4 and 4 from NUM_COLS 5 on RDNA3.** Same grammar as the row above. Startup line: `ggml_vulkan: q6_k mmvq mat-vec rows: rm_int_n (device-probe (rm_int_n, upstream shape)) [GGML_ARIFI_Q6K_MMVQ_ROWS]`. Added 2026-09-21 (lane-258/R74). | Scope: `NUM_ROWS` + workgroup denominator of **`mul_mat_vec_q6_k_q8_1_f32` only**. It exists because `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_WIDE_ROWS_FROM` feeds `rm_int_n`, which every q8_1 mat-vec pipeline in the tree takes (q4_K included, whose width knee is a closed lever), so that knob can measure a q6_K rows arm but can never ship one. | Leave unset for serving; set `1` to reproduce R74's C2 arm. | **Measured REFUTATION, and it names the mechanism.** Equality-proved against the diagnostic knob first: rows 1 reads 111/130/137/144/151/159/154/156 VGPRs, byte-for-byte the `WIDE_ROWS_FROM=8` arm. Timed, rows 1 removes only part of the n=5 MMVQ cliff (21322 → 18787 us on 248320×5120, still behind the f32 shader's 16017) and **destroys the shipped n=7,8 route win** (0.6470 / 0.5590 on lm_head, 0.75 / 0.67 on 5120×17408). So the static 4-row shape is not the cause of the MMVQ width cliff — it is the cause of the n=7,8 win, which is why the R71b admission island survives R74 unchanged. Bit-identical, PROVEN: shipped-rows and rows-1 dumps both sha `85bca201…b6cb78b1` over 81,018,824 bytes. Receipts `r74-evidence/{10-stats-mmvq-q6krows1,21-paired,41-dump-sha256}.txt`; report §0, §2. |
 | `GGML_ARIFI_OP_DUMP` | **Unset = off, and the harness is byte-identical to upstream when it is.** A path turns it on. `tests/test-backend-ops.cpp` only — **never linked into any shipped binary**, so it cannot affect `llama-cli`, `llama-server` or any library consumer. Added 2026-09-18 (lane-249/R61 step 2). | Scope: two things, both test-only. (1) Appends the **test backend's** raw f32 output for every evaluated tensor, in evaluation order, to one file, so two runs of the same filtered sweep differing only in a kernel arm can be compared by `sha256` of the whole file — a bit-identity proof over an entire sweep rather than a per-row tolerance check. (2) **Makes `init_tensor_uniform` deterministic**, because (1) is meaningless without it: upstream seeds a `thread_local` engine from `std::random_device` and splits each tensor across `hardware_concurrency()` slices, so every run draws different data. The deterministic seed is derived from the tensor's own identity (type, shape, name, range), stable against thread scheduling *and* initialisation order. | Use it whenever a change claims to be bit-identical and you want that proven rather than argued — and **always take a same-arm control run**, two runs of the *unchanged* arm, before trusting any cross-arm comparison. | **It is the control that earns this row.** On first use the two legacy control runs disagreed (`a7374122…` vs `cd821d8c…`), exposing the harness nondeterminism above; under that harness a legacy-vs-v2 match was not merely unproven but *impossible*. After the root fix, legacy, control and v2 all read `271472A9…C993` over 79,258,240 bytes spanning n=1..8. The same method then settled the `IQ3_N7_ROWS` question (all four arms sha `9E70CA84…`). **Does not cover `MUL_MAT_ID`**: `init_mul_mat_id_tensors` shuffles its expert `ids` from `std::random_device`, so an `_id` dump would differ run to run whichever arm ran; a source comment says so. The tree already documented this class of nondeterminism twice (the R46b note at the S-X8 `m=1` removal, and `sx8_ref_value`'s "init_tensor_uniform cannot be used here"), so this follows existing practice. Receipt `r61-evidence/44-dump-sha256.txt`; report §2(d). |
 | `GGML_ARIFI_FA_DEQUANT_KV` | **Device default when unset, which is ON on every device** (upstream behaviour). `1` forces the f16 dequant-KV scratch copy ON, `0` forces the in-place quantized-KV read. Added 2026-09-06 (lane-212/R19), default flipped to ON 2026-09-07 (lane-220/R27). | Scope: Vulkan flash attention with a quantized K and V, prefill only (`neq1 >= 64`); decode never reaches it. One stderr receipt line `ggml_vulkan: FA dequant-KV scratch: ON\|OFF (device default\|GGML_ARIFI_FA_DEQUANT_KV)` binds every A/B log to the path it ran. Bit-identical either way - the same FA math with f32 accumulation, only the KV read strategy differs. A device that measures the scratch copy as a cost flips its own default at `fa_deq_device_default`, with its number. | Leave it unset. Set `0` to reproduce the pre-R27 AMD RDNA3 arm on the same binary, or on a device where the scratch copy is measurably a cost. | **The knob and the receipt are the result; the magnitude is NOT.** R19D measured `+8.08%` clean prefill on one binary and R27 flipped the default on it. The R18 wave recomputed the same banked rounds on 2026-09-08 and **it does not reproduce**: gate OFF vs ON on one binary reads decode `+0.49%` CI95 `[-1.62%, +2.59%]` and prefill `+0.85%` `[+0.36%, +1.33%]`; the DFlash2 arm reads decode `-0.91%` `[-1.93%, +0.12%]`. Do not quote `+8.08%`. Evidence: `research/local-inference/lane-evidence/2026-09-02-lane-209/r18-kernel-wave/R18-KERNEL-WAVE-REPORT.md`, `.../r19-seat-prefill/R19D-ENGAGEMENT-LEGS.md`. |
 | `LLAMA_DFLASH_FUSED_INJECT` | **OFF when unset on this fork - the LEGACY encode+decode path. This DIVERGES from upstream 662a0b012, deliberately.** `1` selects the fused injection (upstream shape). Added 2026-09-06 (lane-212/R18), default set to legacy 2026-09-08 (lane-225/R38). The C API default (`llama_cparams::dflash_fused_inject`) stays **fused**, because the legacy shape is a caller contract - the caller must `llama_encode` the chunk first - so only `common/speculative.cpp`, which owns both halves, defaults it off. | Scope: the DFlash2 drafter's K/V injection only. Fused = one `llama_decode` per chunk with the encoder (fc + rms norm) in-graph. Legacy = `llama_encode` the chunk through `graph<true>`, read the encoded rows back through `llama_get_embeddings_nextn`, feed them to the injection decode at the draft width. Nothing removed: `graph<true>`, the fused embd branch and the legacy host path all stay compiled. One receipt line per process names the arm. | Set `1` to restore upstream behaviour, and on any box that has not measured this pair. The default here is a **conservative choice on a sub-floor number, not a measured win** - see the cost cell before treating it as a recommendation. | **DOCUMENTED-TAKE, no gain, and the default flip is under the floor.** Seat DFlash2 decode `-0.98%` CI95 `[-1.16%, -0.80%]` fused vs legacy; U-IQ4XS `+0.05%` `[-0.56%, +0.67%]`; U-Q3KXL `-0.87%` `[-2.50%, +0.77%]`. Against this box's own build floor - the R18 wave's INERT controls, where both arms ran identical code, moved decode by up to **+3.71%** and prefill by up to **-8.28%**, both excluding zero, so **none of these three clears it** and the direction is all the flip rests on. Quality: F-141 both arms on the seat, `test-quantize-fns` identical to the bank. Evidence: `.../r18-kernel-wave/r15c2-dflash-encoder-fusion`. |
